Briefly, the proposed charge reassigning algorithm consists of the following procedure. For each event, the event generator provides a number of charged and neutral pions with their energy-momenta \(p_{i}\) and space-time coordinates \(x_{i}\) at freeze-out. Pion (\(i\)) is chosen randomly and \(+\), \(-\) or 0 charge is assigned to it randomly with weights proportional to \(P_{+}=n_{+}/n\), \(P_{-}=n_{-}/n\) and \(P_{0}=n_{0}/n\), where \(n=n_{+}+n_{-}+n_{0}\) is total multiplicity of pions \(\pi^{+}\), \(\pi^{-}\) and \(\pi^{0}\) in event. This pion defines a new phase-space cell. Distances in momenta \(|q|=|p_{i}-p_{j}|\) between the chosen pion (\(i\)) and all other pions (\(j\)) which are still without signs are calculated and pions (\(j\)) are arranged in order of ascending \(|q|\). The additional pions (\(j\)) are then added to the cell with Gaussian weight [28]

\[P_{ij}=\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2}\,|q^{2}||r^{2}|\right)\,, \tag{4.2}\]

where \(r=x_{i}-x_{j}\) and \(q=p_{i}-p_{j}\) are the space-time distance between two

